# Spring Boot and Java enterprise

## Architecture

- Prefer **convention over configuration** and **standalone**, production-oriented apps.
- Layer clearly: `@RestController` / `@Controller` (web), `@Service` (business), `@Repository` or Spring Data (data), `@Component` for cross-cutting beans.
- Use **starter** dependencies where they match the module; do not add frameworks the module does not already use without a clear need.

## Dependency injection

- Prefer **constructor injection**; avoid `@Autowired` on fields.
- Prefer Lombok-generated constructors (`@RequiredArgsConstructor`) instead of writing constructors manually when the module already uses Lombok consistently.
- Avoid field injection even when using Lombok; keep dependencies as `private final` fields.
- Prefer Lombok (`@Getter`, `@Setter`, `@RequiredArgsConstructor`, `@NoArgsConstructor`, `@AllArgsConstructor`, `@Builder`) over hand-written boilerplate for DTO/entity/service classes, unless custom logic is required.

```java
// BAD — field injection
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orders;
}

// GOOD — constructor injection with Lombok
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderService {
    private final OrderRepository orders;
}
```

## Data access

- Use **Spring Data JPA** (`JpaRepository`, etc.) and Hibernate as the JPA provider when the module uses JPA.
- Put **`@Transactional`** on service boundaries (appropriate propagation/isolation), not scattered on controllers unless justified.
- Prefer **Flyway** or **Liquibase** for schema migrations when the project uses them.

## Configuration

- Use **`application.yml`** / `application.properties`, **Spring profiles** (`dev`, `test`, `prod`).
- Prefer **`@ConfigurationProperties`** for structured, type-safe config; use **`@Value`** only for simple values.
- Keep configuration **externalized** (env, config server, secrets) rather than hardcoding secrets in source.

## Security (Spring Security)

- Model **authentication** and **authorization** explicitly; use **JWT** or **session**-based auth consistently with the module.
- Use **`@PreAuthorize`** / method security when enabled.
- Configure **CORS** and **CSRF** deliberately for the client and API style (e.g. stateless API vs browser form posts).
- Use **OAuth2 / OIDC** when the product integrates with external identity providers.

## Observability

- Expose **health and metrics** via **Spring Boot Actuator** when enabled.
- Use **Micrometer** for metrics export; add **distributed tracing** (Zipkin / OpenTelemetry) where the stack supports it.
- Prefer **structured logging** (consistent fields, correlation IDs) for operations and debugging.

## API and quality

- Centralize errors with **`@ControllerAdvice`** / **`@RestControllerAdvice`**.
- Validate inputs with **`@Valid`** and Bean Validation (`@NotNull`, etc.) on DTOs and controller parameters.
- Add **`@SpringBootTest`** (and test slices where appropriate) for integration coverage when the module has tests.

## Java 21 best practices (required)

- Target **Java 21 LTS** features intentionally; do not mix legacy patterns when a clearer Java 21 option exists.
- Prefer **`record`** for request/response DTOs and immutable value objects; use class only when mutable state or framework constraints require it.
- Use **sealed hierarchies** (`sealed` + `permits`) for closed domain models (states/events/errors) to make `switch` exhaustive and safer.
- Prefer **pattern matching `switch`** over long `if/else instanceof` chains for polymorphic branching.
- Use **`Optional` only for return types**, not entity/DTO fields and not method params.
- Prefer **immutable collections** (`List.of`, `Set.of`, `Map.of`) for constant data; remember `Map.of` rejects null keys/values.
- Do not expose raw `Object`/`Map<String, Object>` in public API contracts unless absolutely necessary; prefer typed DTOs.
- Prefer `ResponseEntity<ConcreteResponse>` over `ResponseEntity<?>` for successful paths to keep API schema stable.
- Use `var` only when the inferred type is obvious from the right-hand side; avoid `var` if it hurts readability.
- Use **`java.time`** only (`Instant`, `OffsetDateTime`, `LocalDateTime`, `ZoneId`); never use legacy `Date`/`Calendar` in new code.
- Always define timezone behavior explicitly at system boundaries (DB, JSON, external API).
- For concurrency-heavy workloads, consider **virtual threads** (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`) only for blocking I/O use-cases; profile before rollout.
- Never swallow exceptions; wrap with context and preserve cause (`new XException(message, cause)`).
- Keep logs structured and safe: no secrets, include correlation identifiers, and avoid logging full payloads with sensitive data.

## Java 21 examples

```java
// GOOD — explicit API contract with record DTO
public record DescribeInstrumentResponse(String status, String model, Object data) {}

// GOOD — exhaustive switch with sealed hierarchy
sealed interface TxType permits Deposit, Withdrawal, Bonus {}
record Deposit() implements TxType {}
record Withdrawal() implements TxType {}
record Bonus() implements TxType {}

String code = switch (txType) {
    case Deposit d -> "deposit";
    case Withdrawal w -> "withdrawal";
    case Bonus b -> "bonus";
};
```
